                    \text{definite status paths in $\g_{\overline{\mb{X} \setminus \{X\}} \underline{X}}$ from $X$ to $\mb{Y}$ that are} \\
                    \text{d-connecting given $\mb{X} \setminus \{X\} \cup \mb{Z}$}
                \end{array}\right\}\text{ and} \nonumber\\
    \mb{S_2} &= \{ \text{ paths in $\mb{S_1}$ with the fewest colliders } \}. \label{thm:alg-complete-s2}
\end{align}
The remainder of the proof falls into two cases: (a) when $\mb{S_2}$ only contains paths that start directed (i.e., of the form $X \gets \dots Y$) and (b) when $\mb{S_2}$ contains paths that start undirected (i.e., of the form $X - \dots Y$). Rather than providing an overview of each case, we offer two examples below. Example \ref{ex:alg-condition1} considers an MPDAG with a path that falls into (a), and Example \ref{ex:alg-condition2} considers an MPDAG with a path that falls into (b). In both examples, we show that the conditional causal effect is not identifiable.

